{"kind":"request","text":"Listing request #80123: ruling needed on the existing read boundary before implementation.\n\nVerified at Main f9aca97f84803860cb10a714b1bf643e50bc87f1: packages/scope/src/scope-index.ts:131–146 can call registerKnown() for an unknown scope in register mode, including read requests. packages/scope/src/object.ts:451–467 and :860 route summary through #first(), which can resume parked operations; #invoke also diagnoses archives and recovers alarms. The CLI cannot promise zero incidental server writes through an existing public read. Existing scopeIndexAccess also acquires/saves sessions and may retry forbidden reads; I will not reuse that path.\n\nConcrete minimal design: validate the saved scope-index manifest locally, then one signed-only bounded summary GET per listed scope, no sessions, fallback, retry, pagination, mutation or registration request. Project only scope, installItem, release, publisher, host. Active Native Rules installation rows carry validated publisher/release/URI; derive host without emitting URI. Pending installations make a scope incomplete, since a created child can precede the active announcement. Unreadable/malformed/unsupported scopes get fixed error codes. A summary proves its full scope reference/native pin but cannot reverify the manifest's genesis hash.\n\nPlease rule whether #80123's no-registration/write condition means no mutation/session/register requests by this command, with existing server read side effects explicitly qualified and operator pre-index/enforce conditions documented; or requires a new strictly read-only server path and an explicit deployment plan. Enforce alone does not remove first-turn resumption. I infer that deploying a new endpoint from Main would itself need the trust readiness this task is intended to establish, so that alternative needs a concrete rollout decision.\n\nAlso confirm locator semantics: propose scope=created application ScopeRef, installItem={scope:owning Rules ScopeRef,item:id}, preserving both references within the five requested fields. The alternative scope=owning Rules ScopeRef plus numeric installItem omits the created application locator.\n\nNo code or live reads yet. R14's already-running gate is unchanged; its review/landing remain behind this listing. I can prepare bounded projection/recorded fixture work while awaiting the ruling.","body":{"conditions":"Explicit ruling on the listing command's no-write boundary and locator semantics; if a strictly read-only endpoint is required, name its deployment plan before the trust-readiness listing can run.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}